/* exports_cuda.map — what liblorrax_ffi.so keeps PRIVATE.
 *
 * A linker version script (ld --version-script).  Everything matched by
 * `local:` is demoted to a local symbol and disappears from `.dynsym`;
 * references to it from inside this library bind at static-link time, so it
 * can be neither interposed by, nor interpose on, anything else in the
 * process.  Everything not matched keeps default visibility.
 *
 * WHAT THIS CLOSES — KNOWN_FAILURES L1, the cross-.so one-definition-rule
 * violation.  liblorrax_ffi.so and liblorrax_ffi_host.so are both dlopened
 * RTLD_GLOBAL into one process (a CUDA-capable process legitimately does
 * both: CUDA handlers on the device, host phdf5 reads on the CPU backend),
 * and ld.so resolves a name to the FIRST object that defined it -- for the
 * whole process, INCLUDING for the later library's own internal calls.
 *
 * MEASURED 2026-08-07 on the BUILD_NOTES-pinned pair, `nm -D --defined-only`:
 * 259 names in common, of which 25 are LORRAX's own -- the nine `lrx_*`
 * ctypes entry points plus sixteen mangled `lorrax_ffi::phdf5::*`
 * (`open_ctx`, `close_ctx`, `ensure_dataset`, `open_dataset_ro`,
 * `ensure_pinned`, `ensure_read_buf`, `ensure_mpi_initialized`, `env_flag`,
 * `~PhdfCtx` x2, the `dt::` HDF5 type singletons and their guards).  Those 25
 * are the defect: `PhdfCtx` has a DIFFERENT LAYOUT in the two builds, so one
 * library's handler read the other's struct at the wrong offsets, which is
 * B1's `offset_base=[0,0,0,4596944070643295330]` -- a float64 as an int64.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHY THIS HIDES `*lorrax_ffi*` AND NOT `*`.  A DENYLIST, DELIBERATELY.
 * ============================================================================
 * The obvious version of this file is `local: *;` with the handler surface
 * and the ctypes ABI listed under `global:`.  MEASURED, Perlmutter
 * 2026-08-08: that form CRASHED five host cells while a third-party C++
 * template library (removed 2026-10-23) was linked, because it localises the
 * weak COMDAT template instantiations our translation units emit, which the
 * C++ ABI intends the dynamic linker to merge with that library's.  So the
 * rule this file states is the rule the defect needs: **LORRAX's own
 * definitions are private to the library that compiled them**; everything
 * else keeps the linkage the C++ ABI intends.  A denylist also fails safe: a
 * new LORRAX symbol in `namespace lorrax_ffi` is hidden automatically, a new
 * third-party dependency is left alone.  Not re-measured without that library.
 *
 * NOT -fvisibility=hidden: gcc then marks a type's typeinfo NAME private, and
 * libstdc++'s `type_info::operator==` compares pointers only, so an exception
 * thrown across a .so boundary would stop matching.  A version script
 * relocalises at LINK time without touching what the compiler emitted.
 *
 * The FROZEN `*Ffi` handler symbols (invariant 1) and the `lrx_*` ctypes ABI
 * are not mentioned here at all: they are not `lorrax_ffi`-namespaced, so
 * they keep default visibility by not matching.  The nine `lrx_*` entry
 * points the HOST leg also compiles are disambiguated by RENAMING rather
 * than by hiding -- Python has to dlsym them -- see cpp/common/c_abi.h.
 */
{
  global:
    /* THE TWO EXCEPTIONS, and the reason this file grew a `global:` clause.
       Until 2026-08-08 the device leg carried no build stamp at all, so
       cpp/common/build_config.cc was host-only and nothing it defined could
       match the denylist below.  It is now in LORRAX_FFI_SOURCES too, and its
       two entry points are named `lorrax_ffi_cuda_*` — which the denylist
       would take.  An exact name beats a wildcard in a version script.

       Both are declared as APIs and both are read from OUTSIDE this library:
       the stamp by `strings`/the acceptance tier, the ABI by both Python
       loaders, at dlopen, before a single handler is declared.  They collide
       with nothing: the host leg's twins carry `_host`, not `_cuda`, so the
       two sanctioned surfaces stay disjoint and check 6's intersection stays
       empty.  Hiding them would not fail anything — it would silently disarm
       the pairing refusal, which is the failure mode
       cpp/common/lorrax_ffi_abi.h exists to remove. */
    lorrax_ffi_cuda_build_config;
    lorrax_ffi_cuda_abi_version;
  local:
    /* every C++ entity in `namespace lorrax_ffi`, including std:: templates
       instantiated on our types, our function-local statics (`_ZZ...`) and
       their guard variables (`_ZGVZ...`). */
    *lorrax_ffi*;
};
